1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Be2 O-O 6. Nf3 e5 7. d5 {This move surprised me but not my opponent who had prepared for John Cooper in this variation before the tournament. Meanwhile identical moves were happening in Cooper-Morris on the adjacent board} a5 8. Bg5 h6 9. Bh4 Na6 10. Nd2 Qe8 11. a3 Bd7 12. b3 {Else 12...a4 fixing the queenside} Nh7 13. Rb1 Nc5 {Encouraging b4 in this way is regarded as suspect; but it is none too easy to exploit} 14. f3 h5 15. Qc2 ({The text, in conjunction with White's next is too slow.} 15. O-O Bh6 16. Qc2 f5 17. b4 axb4 18. axb4 Na4 19. Nb5 Bxb5 20. cxb5 Rf7 21. exf5 gxf5 $2 (21... Rxf5 22. Bd3) 22. f4 $1 {J.Cooper-CF.Morris (not necessarily by this move order)}) 15... Bh6 16. Bf2 f5 17. b4 ({After this, White can at least meet 17...fxe4 with 18 Nxe4 occupying e4 with a piece.} 17. O-O Nf6 18. b4 fxe4 $1 {is very comfortable for Black}) 17... axb4 18. axb4 Na4 19. O-O Nf6 20. Nb5 $5 Bxb5 21. cxb5 fxe4 $2 ({Black should accept the pawn sacrifice with} 21... Bxd2 {and 22...fxe4; the bishops give White practical chances but no more.}) 22. Nxe4 Nxe4 23. fxe4 Qe7 24. Rb3 $1 Kh7 $2 ({Black made a rather ill-timed draw offer with this move. The only way to save the c-pawn is to prepare Na8:} 24... Rae8 25. Ra3 Nb6 26. Rc3 Na8 {but White is obviously better.}) 25. Ra3 Nb6 26. Rc3 Nd7 ({What else?} 26... Rac8 {is impossible.}) ({Editor's note:} 26... Rf7 {may hold.}) 27. Rxc7 Qd8 28. b6 Kg8 29. Qb3 Bf4 30. Qh3 $1 {Black misses his light-squared bishop.} Nf6 31. Qe6+ Kh8 32. Bh4 $1 Nxe4 (32... Ng4 {gets the same treatment}) (32... g5 33. Rxf4 gxf4 34. Qf5 $18) 33. Qxg6 $1 (33. Bxd8 $4 Be3+ 34. Kh1 Rxf1+ 35. Bxf1 Nf2+ {draws.}) 33... Be3+ 34. Kh1 1-0

